      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before 1890 the railroad station was called Twin Wells, named for the two wells that supplied water to workers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        6,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hatton%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After 1901 James Bronson platted Hatton, and the town quickly grew to include four warehouses, five stores, two hotels, a bank and a school.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hatton%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hatton sits in the Channeled Scablands on the west slope of Providence Coulee, a corridor the Northern Pacific used for its main line.",
